      "body": "## Summary\n- `StakingManager's` L1 operations queue contains no recovery mechanism. When an L1 bridge fails, subsequent operations become irreversibly locked, permanently blocking user funds. Any normal user can exploit this with minimal cost `10 gwei`, causing `ALL` users with pending withdrawals to `permanently` lose their funds. \n\n- This attack requires minimal investment (just 10 gwei), affects all users, and cannot be resolved without contract upgrade or admin queue reset, which causes permanent fund loss.\n\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**Root Cause:**\n- When transactions in [_processL1Withdrawals()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-04-kinetiq/blob/main/src/StakingManager.sol#L673) or [_processL1Deposits()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-04-kinetiq/blob/main/src/StakingManager.sol#L721) fail, the contract cannot skip failed operations, reset the queue selectively, retry operations, or override failures.\n\n**Any normal user can exploit this purposely or without no intent**:\n- Staking the minimum allowed amount (10 gwei) => Queuing a withdrawal that creates a tiny L1 operation (1 in 8 decimals) => Permanently blocking all subsequent operations when the bridge rejects the tiny amount\n- Even without a user doing this maliciously, normal operations with tiny amounts cause identical devastating failures. Worsening this issue. \n\n**Fund loss happens because**: \n- When users queue withdrawals, their kHYPE tokens are immediately burned => When the L1 queue gets blocked, the admin's only option is to reset the entire queue => After reset, all pending operations are deleted with no record of them => Users' `ETH` remains trapped in the contract with no standard recovery mechanism => ALL users who had pending withdrawals in the queue `PERMANENTLY LOSE THEIR FUNDS`\n\n### Code Snippet\n```solidity\n// src/StakingManager.sol - _processL1Withdrawals function\nfunction _processL1Withdrawals(uint256 batchSize) internal returns (uint256) {\n    uint256 length = _pendingWithdrawals.length;\n    if (length <= _withdrawalProcessingIndex) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    // Calculate how many operations we can process\n    uint256 endIndex = _withdrawalProcessingIndex + batchSize;\n    if (endIndex > length || batchSize == 0) {\n        endIndex = length;\n    }\n\n    uint256 processedCount = 0;\n\n    // Process withdrawals\n    for (uint256 i = _withdrawalProcessingIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {\n        L1Operation memory op = _pendingWithdrawals[i];\n        require(op.amount <= type(uint64).max, \"Amount exceeds uint64 max\");\n\n        // Send withdrawal to L1 (8 decimals)\n        l1Write.sendTokenDelegate(op.validator, uint64(op.amount), true);\n        \n        // This external call fails permanently with no recovery mechanism\n        // When it fails, the current operation and all subsequent ones become stuck\n\n        // Only call sendCWithdrawal for user withdrawals\n        if (op.operationType == OperationType.UserWithdrawal) {\n            l1Write.sendCWithdrawal(uint64(op.amount));\n        }\n\n        emit L1DelegationProcessed(address(this), op.validator, op.amount, op.operationType);\n        processedCount++;\n    }\n\n    // Update the processing index\n    _withdrawalProcessingIndex = endIndex;\n\n    // If we've processed all operations, reset the queue\n    if (_withdrawalProcessingIndex == length) {\n        delete _pendingWithdrawals;\n        _withdrawalProcessingIndex = 0;\n    }\n\n    return processedCount;\n}\n```\n\nWhen a call to `l1Write.sendTokenDelegate()` fails, the operation reverts and the processing index remains unchanged. Since operations process sequentially, all subsequent operations become permanently stuck.\n\n## Attack (Verified by included POC)\n1. A normal user stakes the minimum amount (10 gwei) and receives kHYPE tokens\n2. User queues a withdrawal, creating a tiny L1 operation (1 in 8 decimals)\n3. L1 bridge rejects the tiny amount (realistic behavior in production bridges)\n4. All subsequent operations are blocked, affecting all users\n5. When admins reset the queue, ALL pending user withdrawal requests lose their funds permanently\n6. There is no built-in mechanism to recover these lost funds\n\n## Impact\nThis vulnerability:\n1. Causes PERMANENT LOSS OF USER FUNDS when the queue is reset\n2. Can be exploited by any normal user with just 10 gwei\n3. Affects ALL users with pending withdrawals\n4. Forces admins to choose between a permanently blocked queue or resetting and losing user funds\n5. Has no built-in recovery mechanism without contract upgrade\n\nSeverity: HIGH - causes permanent fund loss with minimal cost to attacker, affects multiple users, and provides no recovery mechanism.\n\n## Recommended Mitigation\nChange the processing functions to use safe skip functionality and better error handling:\n\n```solidity\n// Add to StakingManager.sol\n/**\n * @notice Allows operator to skip problematic operations in L1 queues\n * @param isWithdrawal Whether to skip in withdrawal queue (true) or deposit queue (false)\n * @param index Index of operation to skip\n */\nfunction skipL1Operation(bool isWithdrawal, uint256 index) external onlyRole(OPERATOR_ROLE) {\n    if (isWithdrawal) {\n        require(index < _pendingWithdrawals.length, \"Invalid index\");\n        // Emit event before removing for auditability\n        emit L1OperationSkipped(\n            isWithdrawal,\n            _pendingWithdrawals[index].validator,\n            _pendingWithdrawals[index].amount,\n            _pendingWithdrawals[index].operationType\n        );\n        \n        // Remove by copying last element to current position\n        if (index < _pendingWithdrawals.length - 1) {\n            _pendingWithdrawals[index] = _pendingWithdrawals[_pendingWithdrawals.length - 1];\n        }\n        _pendingWithdrawals.pop();\n        \n        // Adjust processing index if needed\n        if (index < _withdrawalProcessingIndex) {\n            _withdrawalProcessingIndex--;\n        }\n    } else {\n        // Same logic for deposit queue\n        require(index < _pendingDeposits.length, \"Invalid index\");\n        emit L1OperationSkipped(\n            isWithdrawal,\n            _pendingDeposits[index].validator,\n            _pendingDeposits[index].amount,\n            _pendingDeposits[index].operationType\n        );\n        \n        if (index < _pendingDeposits.length - 1) {\n            _pendingDeposits[index] = _pendingDeposits[_pendingDeposits.length - 1];\n        }\n        _pendingDeposits.pop();\n        \n        if (index < _depositProcessingIndex) {\n            _depositProcessingIndex--;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// use event\nevent L1OperationSkipped(\n    bool isWithdrawal,\n    address indexed validator,\n    uint256 amount,\n    OperationType operationType\n);\n```\n\nAnd minimum amount validation to prevent small transactions from being queued:\n\n```solidity\n// use constant @StakingManager.sol\nuint256 public constant MIN_L1_OPERATION_AMOUNT = 1e6; // Minimum amount for L1 operations (in 8 decimals)\n\n// change _queueL1Operation to include validation\nfunction _queueL1Operation(address validator, uint256 amount, OperationType operationType) internal {\n    // Check minimum amount for L1 operations\n    require(amount >= MIN_L1_OPERATION_AMOUNT, \"Amount too small for L1\");\n    \n    // same\n}\n```",
